# HOW THIS FILE WAS DERIVED
# -------------------------
# Ownership was measured, not assigned. Every percentage below is a share of
# that path's FILE REVISIONS — (commit x file) pairs, not commits — and every
# one of them is reproduced by exactly this command, one path per run:
#
#     git log --full-history --pretty=format:'@@A@@%an' --name-only -- <path> \
#       | sed 's/ \[C\]//' \
#       | awk '/^@@A@@/{a=substr($0,6); next} NF{n[a]++; t++} \
#              END{for (k in n) printf "%5.1f%%  %4d/%d  %s\n", \
#                                      100*n[k]/t, n[k], t, k}' \
#       | sort -rn
#
# Three details of that command are load-bearing. Drop any one of them and the
# figures below do not come out:
#
#   * --full-history. Without it, git's default history simplification prunes
#     commits that reached the path through a merge. That silently changes the
#     answer for five of the paths below — /publish.py reads 50% instead of 70%
#     — and it makes a path's number depend on how many pathspecs you happened
#     to pass in one invocation. With the flag, a path measures the same either
#     way.
#   * File revisions, not commits. A plain `git log --format='%an' | uniq -c`
#     commit count reproduces none of the figures below, because the authors
#     differ sharply in commit size — see /lib/idp_sdk/, where @webarch-ai's
#     share reads 39% by file revisions and 14% by commits. Where a COMMIT count
#     is quoted below it is measured with the same command plus
#     `--full-history --no-merges` and the same sed: --full-history for the
#     reason above, and --no-merges because a merge is one commit but no file
#     revisions, so counting merges would compare two different populations.
#     Without those flags the /lib/idp_sdk/ split reads 64%/10% of 221 rather
#     than 63%/14% of 196.
#   * The `sed`, which merges the corporate-alias git identity
#     `Mansoor [C] Malik` into `Mansoor Malik`. Both carry the same commit
#     email (malmansx@amazon.com), so leaving them split understates that
#     author on every path he touched. The same `[C]` alias form exists for
#     other contributors, which is why the sed is general rather than a
#     one-name special case.
#
# Merge commits contribute no file revisions, since `--name-only` prints no
# diff for them. No path here needs `--follow`: nothing listed below was
# renamed.
#
# Each author was then mapped to a GitHub account from the commit's
# `author.login` as reported by the GitHub API — not from a guess at their
# handle. A path appears below only when BOTH are true:
#
#   1. a named engineer other than the default owner wrote the majority (or a
#      near-majority co-share) of that path's history, and
#   2. that engineer's commits resolve to a real GitHub username.
#
# Five paths — two subsystems and one top-level script, by two engineers — meet
# (1) but not (2) and are therefore deliberately absent. See the "UNASSIGNED"
# note at the bottom and MAINTAINERS.md for the numbers.
#
# HOW GITHUB READS THIS FILE
# --------------------------
# The LAST matching pattern wins, so the broad default-owner line must come
# first and the specific paths after it. Every specific line keeps @rstrahan as
# a co-owner, because "last match wins" would otherwise remove the maintainer
# from the reviewers of his own repository's subsystems.
#
# A review request is only routed to a user who has write access to this
# repository. An entry for someone without write access is silently ignored —
# so if a line below stops routing, check repository access before editing the
# handle. This is a live dependency, not a hypothetical: see the "CODEOWNERS
# routing depends on write access" section of MAINTAINERS.md, and verify with
#
#     gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/collaborators/<handle>/permission --jq .permission
#
# which returns the effective permission, including access derived from a team.
#
# See MAINTAINERS.md for who these people are and what they own, and
# GOVERNANCE.md for how a change actually gets accepted.

